<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dicuil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 825</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dicuil was an Irish monk and scholar who worked at the Carolingian court in the early 9th century. Around 825 he wrote De mensura orbis terrae, a geographical survey of the world, in which he recorded that Irish hermits had lived on islands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>, until driven out by Norse raiders. He also described clerics who had visited Iceland, which he called Thule, around 795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dicuil.jpg" alt="Dicuil" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bibliothèque nationale de France · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BnF_Latin_4806_f25_Dicuil_De_Mensura_Orbis_Terrae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
